## 3. Insulation Core Feedstock (direct tie to FREEZERWALL)

Worth calling out specifically since FREEZERWALL's insulation core is one of the most material-intensive single components in the whole Wall Platform: mineral-fiber board (Stream 2.4) and select polymer composite (Stream 2.3) both feed candidate insulation core material, meaning FREEZERWALL's closed-cell foam/VIP/mineral-fiber options in Section 3 of its own spec could eventually source at least the mineral-fiber option directly from REFORGE output rather than virgin material.

---

## 4. Contamination & Quality Control

This is the part that makes landfill feedstock fundamentally different from clean scrap or ore, and can't be glossed over:

- **Per-stream contamination testing** before any material moves downstream to a structural or food-adjacent (BATHROOMWALL, FREEZERWALL) application — landfill material carries an unknown history, and a wall that's going in a walk-in cooler or a kid's FLOORFORGE room has a much lower contamination tolerance than a structural stud buried in a wall cavity
- **Grade tiering** — not all recovered material is fit for the same downstream use. Structural-grade steel from clean rebar scrap is a different quality tier than steel recovered from mixed unsorted debris; the refinery output should carry an honest grade stamp rather than treating all "recovered steel" as interchangeable
- **Traceable batch logging** — same repair/inspection philosophy as the rest of FORGE canon (a structure should be inspectable and understandable decades later): recovered-material batches should be logged so a downstream failure can be traced back to a specific feedstock batch if something goes wrong

---

## 5. Where This Sits in the Larger Ecosystem

GERALD: REFORGE completes a loop that was previously one-directional: FORGE/Wall Platform/Floor Forge specs so far assumed "structural steel," "insulation core," "polymer composite" as generic material inputs without asking where those specifically come from. REFORGE is the answer — the same zero-waste autonomous-builder logic already established for Azrael (contamination remediation → monument material) and GERALD (ocean plastic → Reef/Island/Ship/Roam builds) now closes the loop on land-based landfill waste feeding directly into the building-materials standard the user has declared canon-wide.

---

## 6. Open Questions

- **Per-landfill feedstock variability** — real landfills vary wildly in composition by region/era/waste-stream history; REFORGE's sort/refine process needs to be robust to that variability rather than tuned to one idealized waste mix
- **Energy cost of refining vs. virgin material** — melting/refining mixed scrap isn't free; a real energy-balance comparison against virgin material extraction is needed before claiming this is unambiguously better, not just assumed
- **Fixed-plant vs. mobile-unit economics** — which deployment mode (roaming GERALD units vs. a stationary REFORGE plant with mobile front-end) makes sense depends on landfill size/density, and hasn't been modeled yet
- **Legal/regulatory landfill-mining framework** — actual landfill mining is a regulated activity in most jurisdictions; this spec is silent on that because it's a real-world permitting question, not an engineering one, but it's a hard dependency for any real deployment
